<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">São Tomé and Príncipe</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Democratic Republic of Sao Tome and Principe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-tome">São Tomé</a> and Príncipe, officially the Democratic Republic of Sao Tome and Principe, is an island country in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-guinea">Gulf of Guinea</a> off the western coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-africa">Central Africa</a>. The islands were uninhabited when Portuguese explorers reached them in 1470. They were settled gradually in the 16th century and became a commercial centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> and cocoa grown on plantations dependent on enslaved Africans. Unrest in the 20th century included riots in 1953 in which several hundred African labourers were killed. After talks in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> in November 1974, the islands became independent on 12 July 1975 as a one-party communist state, which lasted until 1990.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The two main islands lie in the equatorial Atlantic and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-guinea">Gulf of Guinea</a>, about 150 kilometres apart and 250 and 224 kilometres off the north-western coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabon">Gabon</a>. Both belong to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a> volcanic line, which also includes Annobón, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bioko">Bioko</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-cameroon">Mount Cameroon</a>, and formed about 30 million years ago. The climate is tropical, with a rainy season from October to May and rainfall that varies from 7,000 millimetres in the highland cloud forests to 800 millimetres in the northern lowlands. The islands hold many endemic birds and plants, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-tome">São Tomé</a> ibis and the giant sunbird.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first Europeans to put ashore were João de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santarem">Santarém</a> and Pêro Escobar, and the Portuguese judged the uninhabited islands good bases for trade with the mainland. Príncipe was first called Santo Antão and took its present name in 1502, after the Prince of Portugal to whom duties on its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> crop were paid. By 1515 the islands had become slave depots for the coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elmina">Elmina</a>. In the early sugar boom land had little value: when the landowner Álvaro Borges died in 1504, his cleared land and domesticated animals sold for only 13,000 réis, about the price of three slaves.</p><p>At independence Portuguese-owned plantations covered 90 per cent of the cultivated area, and in the early 1900s the islands were the world's largest cocoa exporter, popularly called the Chocolate Islands. A small group of São Toméans formed the Movement for the Liberation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-tome">São Tomé</a> and Príncipe in the late 1950s, based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabon">Gabon</a>.</p><p>After the overthrow of the Caetano dictatorship in April 1974, the new Portuguese regime committed itself to ending its colonies and in November 1974 met the movement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> to agree a transfer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. The first president was its secretary general, Manuel Pinto da Costa. In July 2003 the army seized power for a week, complaining of corruption and of the division of future oil revenues, and an accord returned President Fradique de Menezes to office.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Portuguese is the official and national language, and creoles such as Forro, Angolar and Principense are also spoken. The population descends from people taken to the islands by the Portuguese from 1470 onwards, among them the forros, descended from freed slaves, and the Angolares, reputedly descended from Angolan slaves who survived a shipwreck in 1540. About 201,800 people lived there in 2018, and most belong to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a>. Príncipe has been an autonomous region since 29 April 1995, and the country is a founding member of the Community of Portuguese Language Countries.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “São Tomé and Príncipe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Forced labour took many forms, the harshest shipping workers to <b>São Tomé and Príncipe</b>, from which few returned; in 1954 over 300,000 Angolans were estimated to work under forced labour, which was abolished only in 1962.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-angola">Portuguese Angola · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Portuguese language became, and remains, a major world language with more than 250 million speakers, used officially in Brazil, Angola, Mozambique, Cape Verde, Guinea-Bissau, <b>São Tomé and Príncipe</b>, East Timor and Macau.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Exiles in Equatorial Guinea founded the Marxist Movement for the Liberation of <b>São Tomé and Príncipe</b> (MLSTP) in 1972, intending to create an independent nation.</blockquote><a href="/state/portuguese-sao-tome-and-principe">Portuguese São Tomé and Príncipe</a></li></ul></section>
